--TEST--
Core\Encoding::decodeText names the offset of the first sequence the charset cannot read where iconv() answers false or drops it
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
Reading octets back has the same three `iconv` answers writing them out has, and the same suffix picks between them. Plain `iconv("UTF-8", "UTF-8", "a\xffb")` returns **`false`** and raises a `Notice`, so a caller who did not check gets the empty string where they expected text; `//IGNORE` **drops** the sequence — `iconv("UTF-8", "UTF-8//IGNORE", "a\xffb")` is `"ab"`, with nothing in the result to say a byte was discarded — and the third answer is the one PHP's other decoder gives, `mb_convert_encoding`'s U+FFFD, which is worse than either because it is not an error a caller can notice later at all: a replacement character compares unequal, hashes differently and round-trips to a different value, so the damage surfaces at whatever reads the string next rather than here. `Core\Encoding::decodeText` has one answer and it is the throw. It is a `Fault::thrown` naming the member, the `Core\Charset` case and the **byte offset** of the first sequence that charset cannot read, catchable like any other `Throwable` (`rule:errors/propagation`); `decode_to_string_without_replacement` is what makes it true of the delegated schemes, and `nvs_stdlib::encoding`'s own module doc is where the rule lives. This is AGENTS.md's priority ordering at 2 over 4, the same trade `encodeText`'s own divergence case describes from the other direction. The member that *does* answer a caller who has somewhere to put a `false` is `Core\Encoding::isValidText`, which is this question with the throw taken off rather than a laxer mode of this one — the last row below is both spellings of one question, and it is what makes this a demand for saying which you meant rather than a capability Novis lacks. Two neighbouring facts are agreements rather than divergences and are named here rather than left implied: `Charset::Latin1` refuses nothing at all, because ISO-8859-1 gives all 256 octets a meaning and PHP reads `\x80\x81` as the same two C1 controls, and every sequence both implementations do read is asserted against PHP itself in `encoding-decode-text-matches-iconv`.
--FILE--
<?nvs
final class Refusal {
    // What a caller needs from the throw is the two facts `iconv` reports
    // through none of its modes: which charset could not read the octets, and
    // **where** the sequence it stopped at begins. The wording around them is
    // `nvs_stdlib::encoding`'s own and is not what these rows are about.
    public static function names(string $message, string $case, string $offset): string {
        bool $ok = Core\Str::contains($message, "Core\\Encoding::decodeText()")
            && Core\Str::contains($message, "Core\\Charset::" . $case)
            && Core\Str::contains($message, "at offset " . $offset);
        return $ok as string;
    }
}

uint $refused = 0;

// The row every one of PHP's three answers is wrong about: a stray `0xff` in
// what claims to be UTF-8, which is the shape a truncated or a
// double-converted string arrives in.
try { Core\Encoding::decodeText(Core\Encoding::fromHex("61ff62"), Core\Charset::Utf8); }
catch (Throwable $u) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo "utf8|", Refusal::names($u->message, "Utf8", "1"), "\n"; }

// The high bit in what claims to be ASCII — the commonest call of PHP's own
// `mb_check_encoding`, asked here of the member that answers with the octets.
try { Core\Encoding::decodeText(Core\Encoding::fromHex("6180"), Core\Charset::Ascii); }
catch (Throwable $a) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo "ascii|", Refusal::names($a->message, "Ascii", "1"), "\n"; }

// A UTF-16 subject whose length is not a whole number of code units at all:
// the trailing octet is reported at where it begins rather than the whole
// input being called malformed.
try { Core\Encoding::decodeText(Core\Encoding::fromHex("610063"), Core\Charset::Utf16Le); }
catch (Throwable $odd) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo "utf16le-odd|", Refusal::names($odd->message, "Utf16Le", "2"), "\n"; }

// An unpaired high surrogate, which is a well-formed code unit that is not a
// character — the offset is counted in units *consumed*, so it names the pair
// that failed rather than the character position it would have been.
try { Core\Encoding::decodeText(Core\Encoding::fromHex("61003dd8"), Core\Charset::Utf16Le); }
catch (Throwable $sur) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo "utf16le-surrogate|", Refusal::names($sur->message, "Utf16Le", "2"), "\n"; }

// A multi-byte table truncated mid-character: Shift_JIS reads the first pair
// and stops at the lead byte with no trail byte after it.
try { Core\Encoding::decodeText(Core\Encoding::fromHex("82a082"), Core\Charset::ShiftJis); }
catch (Throwable $jis) { $refused = $refused + 1; echo "shiftjis|", Refusal::names($jis->message, "ShiftJis", "2"), "\n"; }

// Counted rather than read off the lines above: a charset that stopped
// refusing fails here as well as shifting its own row.
echo "refused=", $refused, "/5\n";

// The same question with the throw taken off, which is what a caller who has
// somewhere to put a `false` writes instead — `false` renders as the empty
// string (`rule:types/conversion`), so the refused subject is the short half of this line.
echo Core\Encoding::isValidText(Core\Encoding::fromHex("61ff62"), Core\Charset::Utf8) as string, "|",
     Core\Encoding::isValidText(Core\Encoding::fromHex("616263"), Core\Charset::Utf8) as string, "\n";
--EXPECT--
utf8|1
ascii|1
utf16le-odd|1
utf16le-surrogate|1
shiftjis|1
refused=5/5
|1
